  user_input {
    user_response: "# GOOGLE AI STUDIO MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 MANAGER101\n\n## 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW\n\nBuild a complete, lightweight, offline-first native Android application named **Manager101**.\n\n**App purpose:** A practical reference guide to management models, leadership frameworks, decision-making techniques, business strategy, quality improvement, project management, finance, and productivity.\n\nThe app must be simple, fast, professional, and easy to navigate. It is not a complicated dashboard, social network, AI chatbot, or multimedia learning platform.\n\nThe main experience must be a straightforward, vertically scrollable list of management models. Users can browse the list, tap any model, and open a dedicated page containing its explanation, application steps, a practical example, and one small illustration.\n\n### Core requirements\n\n- App name: Manager101.\n- Platform: Native Android.\n- Programming language: Kotlin.\n- UI: Jetpack Compose with Material 3.\n- Architecture: MVVM with a simple, maintainable structure.\n- Local database: Room, if needed for persistent user data and library indexing.\n- Registration: One-time online registration using Firebase Authentication.\n- Offline access: The entire core model library must be bundled with the app.\n- Illustrations: Exactly one small, relevant illustration per model.\n- Internet: Not required for browsing, searching, reading, or accessing saved local content after initial registration.\n- No AI API calls, remote image loading, streaming, video, or unnecessary background services.\n- No subscription or payment requirement.\n- No advertisements in the initial version.\n- Lightweight APK/AAB with minimal dependencies and efficient memory usage.\n\nDo not add unnecessary features that make the app larger or more complicated than required.\n\n## 2. SIMPLE USER INTERFACE\n\nCreate a clean, professional interface using deep navy blue, emerald green, white, and light gray.\n\n### Home screen\n\nDisplay:\n- Manager101 app name and simple logo.\n- Search bar.\n- A list of the nine management categories.\n- The number of models in each category.\n- A clean, scrollable list of model names within the selected category.\n- A favorite icon beside each model, if favorites are implemented.\n\nThe initial screen should be easy to understand without tutorials.\n\n### Navigation\n\nUse a simple navigation structure:\n\n1. Home \342\200\224 browse all categories.\n2. Library \342\200\224 browse and search all models.\n3. Favorites \342\200\224 view saved models.\n4. Settings \342\200\224 account, theme, privacy, and app information.\n\nUse a compact bottom navigation bar or another simple navigation pattern.\n\nDo not create a large analytics dashboard, complex animations, multiple promotional banners, or unnecessary screens.\n\n### Model list\n\nEach model row should display:\n- Model name.\n- Short one-line description, if space permits.\n- Small category-appropriate icon.\n- Optional favorite button.\n\nAllow users to scroll smoothly through long lists and open a model by tapping its row.\n\nImplement search across all categories, model names, abbreviations, and keywords.\n\n## 3. MODEL DETAIL SCREEN\n\nEvery model must open its own detailed reference page.\n\nDisplay the following:\n\n1. Model name.\n2. Common abbreviation, if applicable.\n3. Category.\n4. One small illustration specific to that model.\n5. Definition.\n6. Purpose.\n7. When to use it.\n8. Step-by-step explanation.\n9. Practical workplace example.\n10. Advantages.\n11. Limitations or common mistakes.\n12. Key takeaway.\n13. Related models, when relevant.\n\nFor quantitative models, include formulas and worked examples.\n\nFor matrix-based models, explain the quadrants and their recommended actions.\n\nFor process-based models, explain the stages in order.\n\nFor management standards and methodologies, distinguish official requirements from general guidance.\n\nKeep explanations concise, accurate, and useful. Avoid excessively long academic essays.\n\nUse original educational content and do not fabricate statistics, official standards, or historical claims.\n\n## 4. ONE SMALL ILLUSTRATION PER MODEL\n\n**This requirement is mandatory.**\n\nEvery unique model in the library must have exactly one small, relevant illustration associated with its detail page.\n\nExamples:\n- SMART Goals: five labeled goal components.\n- Eisenhower Matrix: a compact four-quadrant matrix.\n- SWOT Analysis: a four-quadrant diagram.\n- DMAIC: five connected stages.\n- PDCA: a circular four-stage cycle.\n- Porter\'s Five Forces: five forces surrounding an industry.\n- Balanced Scorecard: four perspectives.\n- Business Model Canvas: a compact nine-block layout.\n- GROW Coaching: four connected stages.\n- Fishbone Diagram: a cause-and-effect diagram.\n- Risk Matrix: likelihood versus impact.\n- Gantt Chart: a compact project timeline.\n- Break-Even Analysis: a revenue and cost intersection chart.\n\n### Illustration implementation\n\n- Use small, lightweight SVG or vector-style illustrations where appropriate.\n- Prefer native Jetpack Compose Canvas drawings, vector resources, or optimized local assets.\n- Illustrations must be stored locally in the app.\n- Do not load images from the internet.\n- Do not download images when the user opens a model.\n- Do not use large photographs, videos, or animated GIFs.\n- Do not create multiple illustrations for the same model.\n- Do not reuse one generic illustration for every model.\n- Each illustration must clearly represent the model it accompanies.\n- Use a consistent visual style, restrained colors, readable labels, and minimal detail.\n- Keep illustrations compact so the written content remains the main focus.\n\nAim for illustrations approximately 240\342\200\223320 dp wide, adapting to the available screen width. Maintain readability and avoid oversized images.\n\nWhere a model is naturally represented by a matrix, flowchart, or graph, use that diagram as its single illustration.\n\nWhere a model is conceptual, create a small original visual summary.\n\nThe app must include the illustration assets or drawing implementations in the project. Do not leave broken image links or blank illustration placeholders.\n\n## 5. COMPLETE REQUIRED MANAGEMENT MODEL LIBRARY\n\nImplement all models listed in the following nine categories.\n\nThe list below is the required content specification. Preserve the category assignments and model names. Some models intentionally appear in multiple categories; they must remain accessible in each relevant category while sharing a single canonical model record and illustration.\n\n### CATEGORY 1 \342\200\224 GENERAL MANAGEMENT AND STRATEGIC PLANNING\n\n1. SMART Goals\n2. Management by Objectives (MBO)\n3. Objectives and Key Results (OKRs)\n4. SWOT Analysis\n5. PESTLE Analysis\n6. Porter\'s Five Forces\n7. Porter\'s Generic Strategies\n8. McKinsey 7S Framework\n9. Balanced Scorecard\n10. Business Model Canvas\n11. Ansoff Matrix\n12. BCG Growth-Share Matrix\n13. Eisenhower Matrix\n14. RACI Matrix\n15. Hoshin Kanri\n16. Stakeholder Analysis\n17. Scenario Planning\n18. Value Chain Analysis\n19. VRIO Framework\n20. Business Process Management (BPM)\n\n### CATEGORY 2 \342\200\224 DECISION-MAKING AND PROBLEM-SOLVING\n\n1. Rational Decision-Making Model\n2. Vroom\342\200\223Yetton\342\200\223Jago Decision Model\n3. Eisenhower Matrix\n4. Pareto Analysis (80/20 Rule)\n5. Five Whys\n6. Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa Diagram)\n7. PDCA Cycle (Plan\342\200\223Do\342\200\223Check\342\200\223Act)\n8. DMAIC (Define\342\200\223Measure\342\200\223Analyze\342\200\223Improve\342\200\223Control)\n9. A3 Problem-Solving\n10. 8D Problem-Solving\n11. Six Thinking Hats\n12. Decision Tree Analysis\n13. Cost\342\200\223Benefit Analysis\n14. Weighted Decision Matrix\n15. Force Field Analysis\n16. Root Cause Analysis (RCA)\n17. Kepner\342\200\223Tregoe Decision Analysis\n18. Impact\342\200\223Effort Matrix\n19. Pre-Mortem Analysis\n20. Cynefin Framework\n\n### CATEGORY 3 \342\200\224 COMMUNICATION, COACHING, AND PEOPLE DEVELOPMENT\n\n1. GROW Coaching Model\n2. CLEAR Coaching Model\n3. OSKAR Coaching Model\n4. SBI Feedback Model (Situation\342\200\223Behavior\342\200\223Impact)\n5. COIN Feedback Model (Context\342\200\223Observation\342\200\223Impact\342\200\223Next Steps)\n6. DESC Communication Model\n7. Johari Window\n8. Radical Candor Framework\n9. Nonviolent Communication (NVC)\n10. Active Listening Framework\n11. 360-Degree Feedback\n12. SMART Development Goals\n13. Individual Development Plan (IDP)\n14. 70\342\200\22320\342\200\22310 Learning and Development Model\n15. Kirkpatrick Four Levels of Training Evaluation\n16. ADDIE Instructional Design Model\n17. Bloom\'s Taxonomy\n18. Kolb\'s Experiential Learning Cycle\n19. Nine-Box Talent Matrix\n20. Skills Gap Analysis\n\n### CATEGORY 4 \342\200\224 OPERATIONS MANAGEMENT, QUALITY, AND PROCESS IMPROVEMENT\n\n1. Lean Management\n2. Six Sigma\n3. Lean Six Sigma\n4. Total Quality Management (TQM)\n5. Kaizen\n6. 5S Methodology\n7. Toyota Production System (TPS)\n8. Just-in-Time (JIT)\n9. Kanban\n10. Theory of Constraints (TOC)\n11. Value Stream Mapping\n12. Business Process Reengineering (BPR)\n13. Statistical Process Control (SPC)\n14. Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)\n15. Poka-Yoke (Error Proofing)\n16. Total Productive Maintenance (TPM)\n17. Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)\n18. SIPOC Diagram\n19. Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)\n20. ISO 9001 Quality Management System\n\n### CATEGORY 5 \342\200\224 PROJECT, PROGRAM, AND PORTFOLIO MANAGEMENT\n\n1. PMBOK Framework\n2. PRINCE2\n3. Agile Project Management\n4. Scrum Framework\n5. Kanban Method\n6. Waterfall Methodology\n7. Critical Path Method (CPM)\n8. Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT)\n9. Earned Value Management (EVM)\n10. Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)\n11. RACI Matrix\n12. MoSCoW Prioritization\n13. RAID Log\n14. Project Risk Matrix\n15. Critical Chain Project Management (CCPM)\n16. Stage-Gate Model\n17. SAFe (Scaled Agile Framework)\n18. Project Portfolio Management (PPM)\n19. Benefits Realization Management\n20. Monte Carlo Simulation for Project Risk\n\n### CATEGORY 6 \342\200\224 PERFORMANCE MANAGEMENT AND PRODUCTIVITY\n\n1. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)\n2. SMART Performance Goals\n3. Objectives and Key Results (OKRs)\n4. Balanced Scorecard\n5. Management by Objectives (MBO)\n6. Performance Prism\n7. Benchmarking\n8. 360-Degree Performance Appraisal\n9. Performance Improvement Plan (PIP)\n10. Leading and Lagging Indicators\n11. Productivity Measurement Model\n12. Logic Model (Inputs\342\200\223Activities\342\200\223Outputs\342\200\223Outcomes)\n13. Performance Dashboard\n14. Service Level Agreement (SLA) Management\n15. Human Capital ROI\n16. Economic Value Added (EVA)\n17. Pareto Principle\n18. Eisenhower Matrix\n19. Personal Kanban\n20. Time Blocking\n\n### CATEGORY 7 \342\200\224 RISK MANAGEMENT, GOVERNANCE, AND COMPLIANCE\n\n1. ISO 31000 Risk Management Framework\n2. COSO Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)\n3. COSO Internal Control Framework\n4. Risk Assessment Matrix\n5. Risk Register\n6. Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)\n7. Bow-Tie Risk Analysis\n8. Fault Tree Analysis\n9. Business Impact Analysis (BIA)\n10. Business Continuity Planning (BCP)\n11. Disaster Recovery Planning\n12. Three Lines Model\n13. Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)\n14. COBIT Framework\n15. NIST Cybersecurity Framework\n16. NIST Risk Management Framework\n17. Control Self-Assessment (CSA)\n18. Root Cause and Corrective Action (RCCA)\n19. Business Continuity Management (BCM)\n20. Third-Party Risk Management\n\n### CATEGORY 8 \342\200\224 FINANCIAL MANAGEMENT AND MANAGERIAL ACCOUNTING\n\n1. Break-Even Analysis\n2. Cost\342\200\223Volume\342\200\223Profit (CVP) Analysis\n3. Activity-Based Costing (ABC)\n4. Variance Analysis\n5. Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB)\n6. Capital Budgeting\n7. Net Present Value (NPV)\n8. Internal Rate of Return (IRR)\n9. Payback Period\n10. Return on Investment (ROI)\n11. Return on Assets (ROA)\n12. Return on Equity (ROE)\n13. DuPont Analysis\n14. Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC)\n15. Economic Value Added (EVA)\n16. Cash Flow Forecasting\n17. Financial Ratio Analysis\n18. Flexible Budgeting\n19. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)\n20. Make-or-Buy Analysis\n\n### CATEGORY 9 \342\200\224 TIME MANAGEMENT AND PERSONAL PRODUCTIVITY\n\n1. Eisenhower Matrix\n2. Pomodoro Technique\n3. Getting Things Done (GTD)\n4. Time Blocking\n5. Time Boxing\n6. Eat That Frog\n7. ABCDE Prioritization Method\n8. Pareto Principle (80/20 Rule)\n9. MoSCoW Prioritization\n10. RICE Prioritization\n11. Impact\342\200\223Effort Matrix\n12. Critical Path Method\n13. Personal Kanban\n14. Ivy Lee Method\n15. Most Important Tasks (MITs)\n16. Weekly Review Method\n17. Deep Work Framework\n18. Work-in-Progress (WIP) Limits\n19. Capacity Planning\n20. Goal Decomposition and Task Breakdown\n\n## 6. MODEL CATALOG AND DUPLICATE HANDLING\n\nThe nine categories above contain 180 category entries, including intentional cross-category repetitions.\n\nImplement the library using canonical model IDs and category associations.\n\nRequirements:\n- Preserve every listed model name and its category placement.\n- Do not delete repeated models from a category simply because they appear elsewhere.\n- Use one canonical record and one illustration for each genuinely identical model.\n- Link the same model to multiple categories when appropriate.\n- Keep related but distinct methods separate.\n- Display the actual number of unique models and the category-specific number of entries.\n- Include a validation report showing all 180 required category entries are present.\n- Report the final unique model count accurately.\n\nDo not claim the library contains 300 or more unique models unless that number has actually been implemented and validated. The supplied list is the mandatory baseline; additional models may be added later if useful and accurate.\n\n## 7. SEARCH AND BROWSING\n\nImplement fast local search across:\n- Model name.\n- Abbreviation.\n- Definition.\n- Category.\n- Keywords.\n- Common workplace applications.\n\nUsers must be able to:\n- Scroll through a category.\n- Open a model.\n- Return to the list without losing their place.\n- Search across all categories.\n- Browse alphabetically.\n- Filter by category.\n- Save favorites.\n- View related models.\n\nKeep search and navigation entirely offline.\n\n## 8. ONE-TIME REGISTRATION AND OFFLINE ACCESS\n\nUse Firebase Authentication for initial online registration and email verification.\n\nInclude:\n- Registration with full name, email, password, and password confirmation.\n- Email verification.\n- Login.\n- Password recovery.\n- Terms of Use and Privacy Policy acceptance.\n- Clear authentication error messages.\n\nAfter successful registration and verification, allow returning users to open Manager101 without repeatedly logging in.\n\nUse secure local session handling and a clearly defined offline-access policy. Do not require a Firebase request every time the app opens or a model is read.\n\nThe complete model catalog and its illustrations must be included in the installed app.\n\nInitial registration, verification, password recovery, and account deletion may require internet access.\n\nDo not store passwords in plain text or falsely mark accounts as verified.\n\nInclude an appropriate account deletion process for Google Play publication.\n\n## 9. LIGHTWEIGHT APP AND PERFORMANCE\n\nKeeping the app lightweight is a top priority.\n\n- Bundle small optimized illustrations.\n- Prefer vector graphics and native drawings for simple diagrams.\n- Use a single compact illustration per unique model.\n- Avoid high-resolution photographs.\n- Avoid video, GIF, Lottie animation dependencies, and unnecessary UI libraries.\n- Do not bundle duplicate illustration files for cross-listed models.\n- Avoid loading all detail screens simultaneously.\n- Use lazy lists for the model catalog.\n- Load detail content only when requested.\n- Keep database queries efficient.\n- Minimize Gradle dependencies.\n- Do not add analytics, crash-reporting SDKs, or other external services unless explicitly justified.\n- Avoid unnecessary Android permissions and background processes.\n\nProvide an estimated release APK/AAB size after building and report the actual size when the build environment allows it. Do not invent a measured size before building.\n\n## 10. GOOGLE PLAY READINESS\n\nPrepare the application for Google Play publication.\n\nInclude:\n- App label: Manager101.\n- Launcher icon.\n- Application ID placeholder and setup instructions.\n- Version code and version name.\n- Appropriate Android SDK configuration.\n- Minimal permissions.\n- Privacy Policy.\n- Terms of Use.\n- About screen.\n- Account deletion workflow.\n- Firebase configuration instructions.\n- Release build instructions.\n- Content and illustration validation.\n\nUse only compatible, supported Android APIs and stable dependencies.\n\nDo not claim publication readiness until the project has been built and tested.\n\n## 11. REQUIRED PROJECT FILES\n\nGenerate the actual Android Studio project with:\n- Gradle configuration.\n- Kotlin source code.\n- Compose screens.\n- Navigation.\n- Model data files.\n- Local illustrations or vector drawing implementations.\n- Search functionality.\n- Firebase Authentication integration.\n- Local favorites persistence, if implemented.\n- Theme resources.\n- Launcher icon resources.\n- Privacy and Terms screens.\n- Unit tests.\n- README with build and Firebase setup instructions.\n\nKeep the source organized and maintainable. Do not place the entire application in one Kotlin file.\n\nDo not use placeholder model pages or broken image links.\n\n## 12. FINAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore declaring the app complete, verify:\n\n1. All nine categories exist.\n2. All 180 specified category entries are present.\n3. Every unique model opens its own detail page.\n4. Every unique model has exactly one associated illustration.\n5. Cross-listed models share the same canonical record and illustration.\n6. Search works offline.\n7. Model content and illustrations are bundled locally.\n8. Registration and returning-user access work as designed.\n9. The app can reopen saved favorites without internet access.\n10. The app builds successfully.\n11. Required tests pass.\n12. The final unique model count and build size are reported accurately.\n\n**Final objective:** Build Manager101 as a lightweight, professional, offline Android management reference app with nine categories, a simple scrollable model list, concise educational detail pages, one small model-specific illustration per unique model, and secure one-time online registration.\n\nPrioritize simplicity, speed, accuracy, readability, and low app size over unnecessary features.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Enable high thinking\"\nicon: \"network_intelligence\"\n---\n\nYou MUST add thinking mode to the app where relevant to handle users\' most complex queries. You MUST use the gemini-3.1-pro-preview model and set `thinkingLevel` to `ThinkingLevel.HIGH`. Do not set `maxOutputTokens`."
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "# GOOGLE AI STUDIO MASTER PROMPT \342\200\224 MANAGER101\n\n## 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW\n\nBuild a complete, lightweight, offline-first native Android application named **Manager101**.\n\n**App purpose:** A practical reference guide to management models, leadership frameworks, decision-making techniques, business strategy, quality improvement, project management, finance, and productivity.\n\nThe app must be simple, fast, professional, and easy to navigate. It is not a complicated dashboard, social network, AI chatbot, or multimedia learning platform.\n\nThe main experience must be a straightforward, vertically scrollable list of management models. Users can browse the list, tap any model, and open a dedicated page containing its explanation, application steps, a practical example, and one small illustration.\n\n### Core requirements\n\n- App name: Manager101.\n- Platform: Native Android.\n- Programming language: Kotlin.\n- UI: Jetpack Compose with Material 3.\n- Architecture: MVVM with a simple, maintainable structure.\n- Local database: Room, if needed for persistent user data and library indexing.\n- Registration: One-time online registration using Firebase Authentication.\n- Offline access: The entire core model library must be bundled with the app.\n- Illustrations: Exactly one small, relevant illustration per model.\n- Internet: Not required for browsing, searching, reading, or accessing saved local content after initial registration.\n- No AI API calls, remote image loading, streaming, video, or unnecessary background services.\n- No subscription or payment requirement.\n- No advertisements in the initial version.\n- Lightweight APK/AAB with minimal dependencies and efficient memory usage.\n\nDo not add unnecessary features that make the app larger or more complicated than required.\n\n## 2. SIMPLE USER INTERFACE\n\nCreate a clean, professional interface using deep navy blue, emerald green, white, and light gray.\n\n### Home screen\n\nDisplay:\n- Manager101 app name and simple logo.\n- Search bar.\n- A list of the nine management categories.\n- The number of models in each category.\n- A clean, scrollable list of model names within the selected category.\n- A favorite icon beside each model, if favorites are implemented.\n\nThe initial screen should be easy to understand without tutorials.\n\n### Navigation\n\nUse a simple navigation structure:\n\n1. Home \342\200\224 browse all categories.\n2. Library \342\200\224 browse and search all models.\n3. Favorites \342\200\224 view saved models.\n4. Settings \342\200\224 account, theme, privacy, and app information.\n\nUse a compact bottom navigation bar or another simple navigation pattern.\n\nDo not create a large analytics dashboard, complex animations, multiple promotional banners, or unnecessary screens.\n\n### Model list\n\nEach model row should display:\n- Model name.\n- Short one-line description, if space permits.\n- Small category-appropriate icon.\n- Optional favorite button.\n\nAllow users to scroll smoothly through long lists and open a model by tapping its row.\n\nImplement search across all categories, model names, abbreviations, and keywords.\n\n## 3. MODEL DETAIL SCREEN\n\nEvery model must open its own detailed reference page.\n\nDisplay the following:\n\n1. Model name.\n2. Common abbreviation, if applicable.\n3. Category.\n4. One small illustration specific to that model.\n5. Definition.\n6. Purpose.\n7. When to use it.\n8. Step-by-step explanation.\n9. Practical workplace example.\n10. Advantages.\n11. Limitations or common mistakes.\n12. Key takeaway.\n13. Related models, when relevant.\n\nFor quantitative models, include formulas and worked examples.\n\nFor matrix-based models, explain the quadrants and their recommended actions.\n\nFor process-based models, explain the stages in order.\n\nFor management standards and methodologies, distinguish official requirements from general guidance.\n\nKeep explanations concise, accurate, and useful. Avoid excessively long academic essays.\n\nUse original educational content and do not fabricate statistics, official standards, or historical claims.\n\n## 4. ONE SMALL ILLUSTRATION PER MODEL\n\n**This requirement is mandatory.**\n\nEvery unique model in the library must have exactly one small, relevant illustration associated with its detail page.\n\nExamples:\n- SMART Goals: five labeled goal components.\n- Eisenhower Matrix: a compact four-quadrant matrix.\n- SWOT Analysis: a four-quadrant diagram.\n- DMAIC: five connected stages.\n- PDCA: a circular four-stage cycle.\n- Porter\'s Five Forces: five forces surrounding an industry.\n- Balanced Scorecard: four perspectives.\n- Business Model Canvas: a compact nine-block layout.\n- GROW Coaching: four connected stages.\n- Fishbone Diagram: a cause-and-effect diagram.\n- Risk Matrix: likelihood versus impact.\n- Gantt Chart: a compact project timeline.\n- Break-Even Analysis: a revenue and cost intersection chart.\n\n### Illustration implementation\n\n- Use small, lightweight SVG or vector-style illustrations where appropriate.\n- Prefer native Jetpack Compose Canvas drawings, vector resources, or optimized local assets.\n- Illustrations must be stored locally in the app.\n- Do not load images from the internet.\n- Do not download images when the user opens a model.\n- Do not use large photographs, videos, or animated GIFs.\n- Do not create multiple illustrations for the same model.\n- Do not reuse one generic illustration for every model.\n- Each illustration must clearly represent the model it accompanies.\n- Use a consistent visual style, restrained colors, readable labels, and minimal detail.\n- Keep illustrations compact so the written content remains the main focus.\n\nAim for illustrations approximately 240\342\200\223320 dp wide, adapting to the available screen width. Maintain readability and avoid oversized images.\n\nWhere a model is naturally represented by a matrix, flowchart, or graph, use that diagram as its single illustration.\n\nWhere a model is conceptual, create a small original visual summary.\n\nThe app must include the illustration assets or drawing implementations in the project. Do not leave broken image links or blank illustration placeholders.\n\n## 5. COMPLETE REQUIRED MANAGEMENT MODEL LIBRARY\n\nImplement all models listed in the following nine categories.\n\nThe list below is the required content specification. Preserve the category assignments and model names. Some models intentionally appear in multiple categories; they must remain accessible in each relevant category while sharing a single canonical model record and illustration.\n\n### CATEGORY 1 \342\200\224 GENERAL MANAGEMENT AND STRATEGIC PLANNING\n\n1. SMART Goals\n2. Management by Objectives (MBO)\n3. Objectives and Key Results (OKRs)\n4. SWOT Analysis\n5. PESTLE Analysis\n6. Porter\'s Five Forces\n7. Porter\'s Generic Strategies\n8. McKinsey 7S Framework\n9. Balanced Scorecard\n10. Business Model Canvas\n11. Ansoff Matrix\n12. BCG Growth-Share Matrix\n13. Eisenhower Matrix\n14. RACI Matrix\n15. Hoshin Kanri\n16. Stakeholder Analysis\n17. Scenario Planning\n18. Value Chain Analysis\n19. VRIO Framework\n20. Business Process Management (BPM)\n\n### CATEGORY 2 \342\200\224 DECISION-MAKING AND PROBLEM-SOLVING\n\n1. Rational Decision-Making Model\n2. Vroom\342\200\223Yetton\342\200\223Jago Decision Model\n3. Eisenhower Matrix\n4. Pareto Analysis (80/20 Rule)\n5. Five Whys\n6. Fishbone Diagram (Ishikawa Diagram)\n7. PDCA Cycle (Plan\342\200\223Do\342\200\223Check\342\200\223Act)\n8. DMAIC (Define\342\200\223Measure\342\200\223Analyze\342\200\223Improve\342\200\223Control)\n9. A3 Problem-Solving\n10. 8D Problem-Solving\n11. Six Thinking Hats\n12. Decision Tree Analysis\n13. Cost\342\200\223Benefit Analysis\n14. Weighted Decision Matrix\n15. Force Field Analysis\n16. Root Cause Analysis (RCA)\n17. Kepner\342\200\223Tregoe Decision Analysis\n18. Impact\342\200\223Effort Matrix\n19. Pre-Mortem Analysis\n20. Cynefin Framework\n\n### CATEGORY 3 \342\200\224 COMMUNICATION, COACHING, AND PEOPLE DEVELOPMENT\n\n1. GROW Coaching Model\n2. CLEAR Coaching Model\n3. OSKAR Coaching Model\n4. SBI Feedback Model (Situation\342\200\223Behavior\342\200\223Impact)\n5. COIN Feedback Model (Context\342\200\223Observation\342\200\223Impact\342\200\223Next Steps)\n6. DESC Communication Model\n7. Johari Window\n8. Radical Candor Framework\n9. Nonviolent Communication (NVC)\n10. Active Listening Framework\n11. 360-Degree Feedback\n12. SMART Development Goals\n13. Individual Development Plan (IDP)\n14. 70\342\200\22320\342\200\22310 Learning and Development Model\n15. Kirkpatrick Four Levels of Training Evaluation\n16. ADDIE Instructional Design Model\n17. Bloom\'s Taxonomy\n18. Kolb\'s Experiential Learning Cycle\n19. Nine-Box Talent Matrix\n20. Skills Gap Analysis\n\n### CATEGORY 4 \342\200\224 OPERATIONS MANAGEMENT, QUALITY, AND PROCESS IMPROVEMENT\n\n1. Lean Management\n2. Six Sigma\n3. Lean Six Sigma\n4. Total Quality Management (TQM)\n5. Kaizen\n6. 5S Methodology\n7. Toyota Production System (TPS)\n8. Just-in-Time (JIT)\n9. Kanban\n10. Theory of Constraints (TOC)\n11. Value Stream Mapping\n12. Business Process Reengineering (BPR)\n13. Statistical Process Control (SPC)\n14. Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)\n15. Poka-Yoke (Error Proofing)\n16. Total Productive Maintenance (TPM)\n17. Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)\n18. SIPOC Diagram\n19. Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs)\n20. ISO 9001 Quality Management System\n\n### CATEGORY 5 \342\200\224 PROJECT, PROGRAM, AND PORTFOLIO MANAGEMENT\n\n1. PMBOK Framework\n2. PRINCE2\n3. Agile Project Management\n4. Scrum Framework\n5. Kanban Method\n6. Waterfall Methodology\n7. Critical Path Method (CPM)\n8. Program Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT)\n9. Earned Value Management (EVM)\n10. Work Breakdown Structure (WBS)\n11. RACI Matrix\n12. MoSCoW Prioritization\n13. RAID Log\n14. Project Risk Matrix\n15. Critical Chain Project Management (CCPM)\n16. Stage-Gate Model\n17. SAFe (Scaled Agile Framework)\n18. Project Portfolio Management (PPM)\n19. Benefits Realization Management\n20. Monte Carlo Simulation for Project Risk\n\n### CATEGORY 6 \342\200\224 PERFORMANCE MANAGEMENT AND PRODUCTIVITY\n\n1. Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)\n2. SMART Performance Goals\n3. Objectives and Key Results (OKRs)\n4. Balanced Scorecard\n5. Management by Objectives (MBO)\n6. Performance Prism\n7. Benchmarking\n8. 360-Degree Performance Appraisal\n9. Performance Improvement Plan (PIP)\n10. Leading and Lagging Indicators\n11. Productivity Measurement Model\n12. Logic Model (Inputs\342\200\223Activities\342\200\223Outputs\342\200\223Outcomes)\n13. Performance Dashboard\n14. Service Level Agreement (SLA) Management\n15. Human Capital ROI\n16. Economic Value Added (EVA)\n17. Pareto Principle\n18. Eisenhower Matrix\n19. Personal Kanban\n20. Time Blocking\n\n### CATEGORY 7 \342\200\224 RISK MANAGEMENT, GOVERNANCE, AND COMPLIANCE\n\n1. ISO 31000 Risk Management Framework\n2. COSO Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)\n3. COSO Internal Control Framework\n4. Risk Assessment Matrix\n5. Risk Register\n6. Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)\n7. Bow-Tie Risk Analysis\n8. Fault Tree Analysis\n9. Business Impact Analysis (BIA)\n10. Business Continuity Planning (BCP)\n11. Disaster Recovery Planning\n12. Three Lines Model\n13. Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC)\n14. COBIT Framework\n15. NIST Cybersecurity Framework\n16. NIST Risk Management Framework\n17. Control Self-Assessment (CSA)\n18. Root Cause and Corrective Action (RCCA)\n19. Business Continuity Management (BCM)\n20. Third-Party Risk Management\n\n### CATEGORY 8 \342\200\224 FINANCIAL MANAGEMENT AND MANAGERIAL ACCOUNTING\n\n1. Break-Even Analysis\n2. Cost\342\200\223Volume\342\200\223Profit (CVP) Analysis\n3. Activity-Based Costing (ABC)\n4. Variance Analysis\n5. Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB)\n6. Capital Budgeting\n7. Net Present Value (NPV)\n8. Internal Rate of Return (IRR)\n9. Payback Period\n10. Return on Investment (ROI)\n11. Return on Assets (ROA)\n12. Return on Equity (ROE)\n13. DuPont Analysis\n14. Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC)\n15. Economic Value Added (EVA)\n16. Cash Flow Forecasting\n17. Financial Ratio Analysis\n18. Flexible Budgeting\n19. Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)\n20. Make-or-Buy Analysis\n\n### CATEGORY 9 \342\200\224 TIME MANAGEMENT AND PERSONAL PRODUCTIVITY\n\n1. Eisenhower Matrix\n2. Pomodoro Technique\n3. Getting Things Done (GTD)\n4. Time Blocking\n5. Time Boxing\n6. Eat That Frog\n7. ABCDE Prioritization Method\n8. Pareto Principle (80/20 Rule)\n9. MoSCoW Prioritization\n10. RICE Prioritization\n11. Impact\342\200\223Effort Matrix\n12. Critical Path Method\n13. Personal Kanban\n14. Ivy Lee Method\n15. Most Important Tasks (MITs)\n16. Weekly Review Method\n17. Deep Work Framework\n18. Work-in-Progress (WIP) Limits\n19. Capacity Planning\n20. Goal Decomposition and Task Breakdown\n\n## 6. MODEL CATALOG AND DUPLICATE HANDLING\n\nThe nine categories above contain 180 category entries, including intentional cross-category repetitions.\n\nImplement the library using canonical model IDs and category associations.\n\nRequirements:\n- Preserve every listed model name and its category placement.\n- Do not delete repeated models from a category simply because they appear elsewhere.\n- Use one canonical record and one illustration for each genuinely identical model.\n- Link the same model to multiple categories when appropriate.\n- Keep related but distinct methods separate.\n- Display the actual number of unique models and the category-specific number of entries.\n- Include a validation report showing all 180 required category entries are present.\n- Report the final unique model count accurately.\n\nDo not claim the library contains 300 or more unique models unless that number has actually been implemented and validated. The supplied list is the mandatory baseline; additional models may be added later if useful and accurate.\n\n## 7. SEARCH AND BROWSING\n\nImplement fast local search across:\n- Model name.\n- Abbreviation.\n- Definition.\n- Category.\n- Keywords.\n- Common workplace applications.\n\nUsers must be able to:\n- Scroll through a category.\n- Open a model.\n- Return to the list without losing their place.\n- Search across all categories.\n- Browse alphabetically.\n- Filter by category.\n- Save favorites.\n- View related models.\n\nKeep search and navigation entirely offline.\n\n## 8. ONE-TIME REGISTRATION AND OFFLINE ACCESS\n\nUse Firebase Authentication for initial online registration and email verification.\n\nInclude:\n- Registration with full name, email, password, and password confirmation.\n- Email verification.\n- Login.\n- Password recovery.\n- Terms of Use and Privacy Policy acceptance.\n- Clear authentication error messages.\n\nAfter successful registration and verification, allow returning users to open Manager101 without repeatedly logging in.\n\nUse secure local session handling and a clearly defined offline-access policy. Do not require a Firebase request every time the app opens or a model is read.\n\nThe complete model catalog and its illustrations must be included in the installed app.\n\nInitial registration, verification, password recovery, and account deletion may require internet access.\n\nDo not store passwords in plain text or falsely mark accounts as verified.\n\nInclude an appropriate account deletion process for Google Play publication.\n\n## 9. LIGHTWEIGHT APP AND PERFORMANCE\n\nKeeping the app lightweight is a top priority.\n\n- Bundle small optimized illustrations.\n- Prefer vector graphics and native drawings for simple diagrams.\n- Use a single compact illustration per unique model.\n- Avoid high-resolution photographs.\n- Avoid video, GIF, Lottie animation dependencies, and unnecessary UI libraries.\n- Do not bundle duplicate illustration files for cross-listed models.\n- Avoid loading all detail screens simultaneously.\n- Use lazy lists for the model catalog.\n- Load detail content only when requested.\n- Keep database queries efficient.\n- Minimize Gradle dependencies.\n- Do not add analytics, crash-reporting SDKs, or other external services unless explicitly justified.\n- Avoid unnecessary Android permissions and background processes.\n\nProvide an estimated release APK/AAB size after building and report the actual size when the build environment allows it. Do not invent a measured size before building.\n\n## 10. GOOGLE PLAY READINESS\n\nPrepare the application for Google Play publication.\n\nInclude:\n- App label: Manager101.\n- Launcher icon.\n- Application ID placeholder and setup instructions.\n- Version code and version name.\n- Appropriate Android SDK configuration.\n- Minimal permissions.\n- Privacy Policy.\n- Terms of Use.\n- About screen.\n- Account deletion workflow.\n- Firebase configuration instructions.\n- Release build instructions.\n- Content and illustration validation.\n\nUse only compatible, supported Android APIs and stable dependencies.\n\nDo not claim publication readiness until the project has been built and tested.\n\n## 11. REQUIRED PROJECT FILES\n\nGenerate the actual Android Studio project with:\n- Gradle configuration.\n- Kotlin source code.\n- Compose screens.\n- Navigation.\n- Model data files.\n- Local illustrations or vector drawing implementations.\n- Search functionality.\n- Firebase Authentication integration.\n- Local favorites persistence, if implemented.\n- Theme resources.\n- Launcher icon resources.\n- Privacy and Terms screens.\n- Unit tests.\n- README with build and Firebase setup instructions.\n\nKeep the source organized and maintainable. Do not place the entire application in one Kotlin file.\n\nDo not use placeholder model pages or broken image links.\n\n## 12. FINAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore declaring the app complete, verify:\n\n1. All nine categories exist.\n2. All 180 specified category entries are present.\n3. Every unique model opens its own detail page.\n4. Every unique model has exactly one associated illustration.\n5. Cross-listed models share the same canonical record and illustration.\n6. Search works offline.\n7. Model content and illustrations are bundled locally.\n8. Registration and returning-user access work as designed.\n9. The app can reopen saved favorites without internet access.\n10. The app builds successfully.\n11. Required tests pass.\n12. The final unique model count and build size are reported accurately.\n\n**Final objective:** Build Manager101 as a lightweight, professional, offline Android management reference app with nine categories, a simple scrollable model list, concise educational detail pages, one small model-specific illustration per unique model, and secure one-time online registration.\n\nPrioritize simplicity, speed, accuracy, readability, and low app size over unnecessary features.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
  }
}
