  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/design_guidelines/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 83
    content: "---\nname: \"design-guidelines\"\ndescription: >-\n  Provides design guidelines for building polished, distinctive Android interfaces with Jetpack Compose.\n  Use when building any Android app, screen, component, or user-facing UI. Also use when styling,\n  beautifying, or improving the visual design of existing Android code. Helps the agent generate creative,\n  production-grade code and Material 3 layouts that avoid generic \"AI slop\" aesthetics.\n---\n\nThis skill guides the creation of distinctive, high-fidelity Jetpack Compose\ninterfaces. Implement real working Kotlin code with exceptional attention to\nvisual polish, modern typography, and creative layout choices that avoid generic\nAI-generated defaults.\n\n## Creative Intent\n\nBefore coding, establish a clear, cohesive visual direction:\n\n*   **Select a Theme:** Choose one intentional vibe (e.g., clean minimal,\n    premium dark, playful, warm organic) and ensure all typography, shapes, and\n    colors strictly align with it.\n*   **Avoid generic AI defaults:** Do not use plain gray backgrounds, default\n    system fonts, or unstyled card lists.\n*   **Restraint & Precision:** Good design comes from precision, not complexity.\n    Bold minimalism and striking layout asymmetry outperform cluttered visual\n    accents.\n\n## Creative Direction\n\nAvoid generic AI slop (system font defaults, purple-on-white gradients, or gray\ncards). Commit to a bold aesthetic (e.g., minimal, brutalist, luxury dark,\nplayful):\n\n*   **Asymmetry & Depth:** Use overlapping layers (`Box` + `offset`) and\n    grid-breaking elements.\n*   **Atmosphere:** Use linear/radial gradient brushes or custom Canvas drawings\n    (`Modifier.drawBehind`) instead of solid flat colors.\n\n## Android Styling Guidelines\n\n*   **Typography:** Pair distinct display fonts (Google Fonts bundled locally in\n    `res/font/` via `font-util`; see `android-typography` skill) with a clean\n    body font. Always use `sp` for accessibility scaling.\n*   **Colors & Themes:** Use semantic tokens (`MaterialTheme.colorScheme`) in\n    `Theme.kt`. Support dark theme if desired. Prefer dominant colors with sharp\n    accents.\n*   **Empty States:** Display placeholder icons with a friendly tip for empty\n    lists/charts to enhance first-launch UX.\n*   **NavigationBar:** Prefer standard M3 navigation bars with consistent active\n    pill indicators.\n*   **Iconography:** Prefer standard M3 Icons. Choose active/inactive styles\n    (filled vs outlined) consistently.\n*   **Visual Feedback:** Always provide ripple feedback on clickable components\n    (`Modifier.clickable`) to avoid \"dead\" taps.\n*   **Motion:** Prefer spring animations (`spring()`) and staggered reveals\n    (`AnimatedVisibility`) for transitions under 300ms.\n*   **Surface Depth:** Use M3 `tonalElevation` and semi-transparent layered\n    cards to establish visual hierarchy.\n\n## Technical Implementation Rules\n\n*   **Notch & Navigation Bar Safe Areas:**\n    *   Always call `enableEdgeToEdge()` to support full-bleed content.\n    *   To avoid camera notch clipping at the top, apply\n        `WindowInsets.safeDrawing` padding or status bar insets.\n    *   To avoid system navigation bar (gesture pill or 3-button nav) overlap at\n        the bottom, ensure navigation components (e.g., standard M3\n        `NavigationBar`) respect `WindowInsets.navigationBars`.\n    *   If implementing a **custom bottom navigation** bar, row, or floating\n        bar, you **MUST** explicitly apply\n        `.windowInsetsPadding(WindowInsets.navigationBars)` or\n        `.navigationBarsPadding()` to avoid cutting off bottom icons, active\n        pills, or text labels.\n    *   Ensure parent layouts do not prematurely consume bottom/navigation\n        insets, preventing child components from automatically adjusting.\n*   **Tappable Targets:** Enforce minimum touch target of 48.dp size using\n    `minimumInteractiveComponentSize()`.\n*   **Compose Dimension Extensions (.dp):** When specifying layout dimensions\n    (width, height, spacing, padding, etc.) in Jetpack Compose (Kotlin) code,\n    you must use the `.dp` extension (e.g., `8.dp`, `24.dp`, `2.5.dp`).\n*   **State & Data:** Use MVVM (`ViewModel` + `MutableStateFlow`) with a\n    Repository layer backed by the appropriate persistence engine per the data\n    persistence guidelines.\n"
    num_lines: 84
    num_bytes: 4291
  }
}
