  view_file {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///skills/system_skills/firebase_firestore_android/SKILL.md"
    end_line: 774
    content: "---\nname: \"firebase-firestore-android\"\ndescription: |\n  Integrates Firebase Firestore and Firebase Authentication into Android apps.\n  Covers schema design, zero-trust security rules, local rules testing against\n  emulators, Google Sign-In via Jetpack Credential Manager, and the Firestore SDK\n  data layer (repositories, snapshot listeners, lifecycle).\n\n  Use this skill when:\n  * Adding a database, persistent storage, or user authentication to an app.\n  * Designing a Firestore schema for a new feature.\n  * Generating or auditing Firestore security rules.\n  * Debugging \"Missing or insufficient permissions\" or other Firebase failures.\n  * Adding Firebase initialization or data fetching code to an Android app.\n\n  Do NOT use this skill for:\n  * AI, Gemini, or generative features \342\200\224 use `firebase-ai-logic-android`.\n  * General GCP resource management outside the provided Firebase tools.\n  * Apps that do not use, or intend to use, Firebase as their backend.\n---\n\n# Firebase Firestore & Auth Setup\n\nThese instructions outline the mandatory steps for integrating Firebase\nFirestore and Firebase Authentication into Android applications using modern\nAndroid practices and zero-trust security.\n\n### Prerequisites & Firebase Provisioning\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **MANDATORY BACKEND PROVISIONING VIA `set_up_firebase`**\n>\n> You **MUST** ensure `set_up_firebase` has been executed with `\"firestore\"` in\n> `requestedProducts` (following the 2-step handshake in\n> `firebase-integration-android`).\n>\n> **Tool Capabilities & Limitations**: The `set_up_firebase` tool in this\n> environment is strictly limited to configuring Firestore Enterprise edition\n> and Firebase Auth with the Google Sign-In identity provider ONLY. These should\n> be used whenever reasonable. If the user very explicitly requests a product or\n> auth method that cannot be provided by these (such as Firebase Storage, push\n> notifications via Firebase Cloud Messaging, Anonymous Auth, or Email/Password\n> Auth), the agent should direct the user to the Firebase console to manually\n> take those configuration actions.\n>\n> **Why this is required:** Calling `set_up_firebase` with `\"firestore\"`\n> provisions the Firestore database and sets up security rules on the backend.\n>\n> **Incremental Setup:** If Firebase was already configured previously (e.g.,\n> for AI Logic or Auth), calling `set_up_firebase` with `requestedProducts:\n> [\"firestore\"]` (or `[\"ai_logic\", \"firestore\"]`) is still required to provision\n> Firestore and set up security rules on the backend. `set_up_firebase` is\n> additive and non-destructive\342\200\224it will not undo or overwrite existing AI Logic\n> configurations or resources.\n\n## 1. Implementation Checklist (Execute in Strict Sequential Order)\n\nFollow this phased checklist sequentially for all Firebase applications,\ncompleting each phase prior to developing UI or gameplay code:\n\n-   [ ] **Phase 1: Authentication Foundation (Strict Invariant)**\n\n    -   [ ] **Pre-requisite Check**: Confirm `set_up_firebase` (with\n        `userConfirmedTermsAcceptedInUI: true` and `\"firestore\"` in\n        `requestedProducts`) was completed via the integration skill\n    -   [ ] **Co-Presence Check**: If AI Logic is also requested, invoke\n        `view_file` on `../firebase-ai-logic-android/references/app-check.md`\n        and complete every requirement in it. This is the full App Check\n        contract, not just the `configureAppCheck(context, intent)` helper \342\200\224 the\n        `MainActivity` call-site ordering and passing `intent` are equally\n        mandatory. `configureAppCheck` is a project helper function documented\n        there and is not an SDK built-in.\n    -   [ ] View\n        [references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md) via\n        `view_file` for Auth lifecycle and Credential Manager setup\n    -   [ ] Implement Google Sign-In via `CredentialManager`\n        (`GetSignInWithGoogleOption` / `GetSignInWithGoogleOption.Builder`)\n        using `context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)` with a visible\n        \"Sign in with Google\" button. Even when automated, silent, or\n        low-friction authentication is used, you MUST provide an interactive\n        Google Sign-In fallback. In application code (`app/src/main/`), NEVER\n        use anonymous auth (`signInAnonymously()`), NEVER use email/password\n        auth (`signInWithEmailAndPassword()`), and NEVER omit authentication.\n    -   [ ] Add Composable Auth Gate (`if (user == null) SignInScreen() else\n        AppContent()`) with a visible \"Sign in with Google\" button\n    -   [ ] Auth-gate all collection queries and snapshot listeners (never query\n        Firestore or instantiate feature ViewModels while `auth.currentUser ==\n        null`)\n    -   [ ] Implement fail-fast `requireUserId()` on repository write methods\n        (`auth.currentUser?.uid ?: error(...)`); delegate read query access\n        control to security rules\n    -   [ ] **Authentication Verification**: Confirm `signInAnonymously()` is\n        NOT present in the codebase, and `signInWithEmailAndPassword()` is NOT\n        used anywhere in `app/src/main/` (use only Google Sign-In via Credential\n        Manager)\n\n-   [ ] **Phase 2: Schema, Security Rules & Local Testing (Strict Sequential\n    Order)**\n\n    -   [ ] View\n        [references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md)\n        via `view_file` for schema models & 8 Pillars of Hardened Rules\n    -   [ ] Write `firebase-blueprint.json` schema to workspace root\n    -   [ ] Write `firestore.rules` enforcing `request.auth != null`,\n        `request.auth.uid == userId`, and `isValid[Entity]()`\n    -   [ ] Configure `firebase.json` with an array binding `firestore.rules` to\n        the named database ID from\n        `app/src/main/res/values/firebase_applet_config.xml`\n        (`firestore_database_id`) so the local emulator enforces rules on the\n        named database rather than defaulting to open rules\n    -   [ ] Add `*-debug.log` to `.gitignore` to exclude ephemeral CLI logs\n        (`firestore-debug.log`, `firebase-debug.log`)\n    -   [ ] View\n        [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)\n        via `view_file` for the emulator start command and Robolectric\n        integration testing patterns\n    -   [ ] **Start the Firestore and Auth emulators once** (they are NOT\n        running by default) using the detached start command in\n        `references/firestore-rules-testing.md`, and confirm it prints\n        `Emulators ready`\n    -   [ ] Author and execute JavaScript rules tests first\n        (`firestore.test.js`) via `node --test firestore.test.js` for rapid\n        (~1s) validation of security rules syntax and boundaries\n    -   [ ] Implement Kotlin Data Models and Repositories (`*Repository.kt`)\n    -   [ ] Author Robolectric integration tests ending in `RuleTest` (e.g.,\n        `*RepositoryRuleTest.kt` or `*RuleTest.kt`) under `app/src/test/java/`\n        exercising repository methods against local emulators, including\n        negative tests for unauthenticated callers (`auth.signOut()`) and\n        unauthorized cross-user access\n    -   [ ] Configure `tasks.withType<Test> { failFast = true }` in\n        `app/build.gradle.kts`\n    -   [ ] Execute local Kotlin rules tests (after JavaScript rules validate)\n        against the warm emulators: `gradle :app:testDebugUnitTest --tests\n        \'*Rule*\'`\n    -   [ ] **Verification Gate**: Confirm all rules tests (JavaScript and\n        Kotlin) pass locally before deploying\n    -   [ ] Deploy verified rules via `deploy_firebase` tool (MANDATORY: Always\n        invoke `deploy_firebase` to deploy rules to the backend)\n    -   [ ] Initialize Firestore with custom DB ID:\n        `FirebaseFirestore.getInstance(context.getString(R.string.firestore_database_id))`\n        (MANDATORY: NEVER use the default `(default)` instance or omit the\n        database ID)\n    -   [ ] \342\232\240\357\270\217 **VERIFICATION**: Confirm parameterless `Firebase.firestore` and\n        parameterless `FirebaseFirestore.getInstance()` are NOT present anywhere\n        in the codebase, and all Firestore client references explicitly pass\n        `context.getString(R.string.firestore_database_id)`\n\n-   [ ] **Phase 3: ViewModels, Compose UI & Error Handling**\n\n    -   [ ] View\n        [references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md](references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md)\n        via `view_file` for structured error handling (`handleFirestoreError`)\n        and query best practices\n    -   [ ] Scope data operations to authenticated user ID\n        (`whereEqualTo(\"userId\", uid)` or `/users/{uid}/...`)\n    -   [ ] Build ViewModels, screen state, and domain features\n\n-   [ ] **Phase 4: Mechanical Verification Gate (MANDATORY BEFORE TURN END)**\n\n    -   [ ] Run a search across **application code only** to verify zero\n        unauthorized auth patterns: `grep -rnE\n        \"signInAnonymously|signInWithEmailAndPassword|\\.apps\\.googleusercontent\\.com\"\n        app/src/main/` *(Or invoke `grep_search` with Query\n        `signInAnonymously|signInWithEmailAndPassword|\\.apps\\.googleusercontent\\.com`\n        across `app/src/main`)*\n    -   [ ] \342\232\240\357\270\217 **Scope to `app/src/main/`, never `app/src/`**: the mandated\n        `FirestoreEmulatorTestBase` harness under `app/src/test/` legitimately\n        calls `signInWithEmailAndPassword()` to authenticate against the local\n        Auth emulator (Credential Manager cannot run headless). Searching\n        `app/src/` flags that harness and produces a false failure.\n    -   [ ] \342\232\240\357\270\217 **GATE REQUIREMENT**: Search must return **0 matches**. If any\n        match is found, remediate before concluding:\n        *   `signInAnonymously` / `signInWithEmailAndPassword` matches \342\206\222\n            Transition UI to unauthenticated state + Google Sign-In button flow.\n        *   Hardcoded `*.apps.googleusercontent.com` strings \342\206\222 Replace with\n            `context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2. Progressive Disclosure Reference Directory (Specialized Guides)\n\nRead each reference at the point indicated in the Phase checklist above:\n\n-   [references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md) \342\200\224 SDK\n    initialization, Auth lifecycle, Credential Manager, ViewModel state\n    integration, real-time snapshot listeners, and type-safe data models.\n-   [references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md)\n    \342\200\224 `firebase-blueprint.json` intermediate representation schema, the 8\n    Pillars of Hardened Security Rules, and the Red Team audit.\n-   [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)\n    \342\200\224 emulator and `firebase.json` configuration, `firestore.test.js`, and\n    `FirestoreEmulatorTestBase`. **Mandatory before deploying or updating any\n    Firestore security rules.**\n-   [references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md](references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md)\n    \342\200\224 the structured JSON exception handler (`handleFirestoreError`), query best\n    practices, and quota limits.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 3. Core Architecture Invariants (READ FIRST)\n\n1.  **Google Sign-In (REQUIRED)**: Authenticate via Jetpack `CredentialManager`\n    using `GetSignInWithGoogleOption` with\n    `context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)`. This provider is\n    provisioned automatically by `set_up_firebase` (registering SHA-1/SHA-256\n    and generating `default_web_client_id`). Gate feature UI behind Google\n    Sign-In for all applications.\n2.  **No Auth Fallbacks (PROHIBITED)**: In application code (`app/src/main/`),\n    never call `signInAnonymously()` or `signInWithEmailAndPassword()` \342\200\224 not\n    even as a silent, automated, or low-friction fallback. Always expose an\n    interactive \"Sign in with Google\" button instead.\n3.  **Custom Database ID**: Pass\n    `context.getString(R.string.firestore_database_id)` to\n    `FirebaseFirestore.getInstance(...)` across all Repositories and ViewModels.\n4.  **Multi-User Isolation**: Scope collections to `userId: String` matching\n    `request.auth.uid` or nest under `/users/{userId}/...`, and apply per-user\n    checks on every collection.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4. Dependencies (Pre-configured in Template)\n\nFirebase BoM, Firestore, Auth, Jetpack Credential Manager, and GoogleId are\n**already pre-configured** in `gradle/libs.versions.toml` and\n`app/build.gradle.kts`.\n\nSimply ensure the following pre-configured dependencies are active/uncommented\nin `app/build.gradle.kts`:\n\n```kotlin\n// app/build.gradle.kts\ndependencies {\n    implementation(platform(libs.firebase.bom)) // Already active in template\n    implementation(libs.firebase.firestore)\n    implementation(libs.firebase.auth)\n    implementation(libs.androidx.credentials)\n    implementation(libs.androidx.credentials.play.services)\n    implementation(libs.googleid)\n}\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 5. Mandatory Implementation Workflow\n\nBefore generating or modifying any UI or Kotlin source files, follow these exact\nsteps in order:\n\n### Step 1: Scaffolding, Security Rules & Local Unit Testing\n\n1.  `firebase-blueprint.json` \342\200\224 Create the intermediate representation schema\n    describing the Firestore data models and collections at the workspace root\n    (`/firebase-blueprint.json`). Write it directly using `write_to_file` with\n    only workspace parameters (`TargetFile`, `CodeContent`, `Overwrite`,\n    `Description`, `toolAction`, `toolSummary`). Omit `ArtifactMetadata` to\n    write directly to the workspace root.\n2.  `firestore.rules` \342\200\224 **Before writing to this file, view\n    [references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md](references/firestore-rules-and-blueprint.md)\n    using `view_file`.** Generate security rules corresponding to the blueprint\n    at the workspace root (`/firestore.rules`) using `write_to_file` (omitting\n    `ArtifactMetadata`). Ensure every collection enforces schema validation\n    (`isValid[Entity](request.resource.data)`) and authenticated user ownership\n    access constraints.\n3.  **Local repository and rules integration testing**: Before deploying rules,\n    write and execute local integration tests. Author a repository rule test\n    ending in `RuleTest` or `RulesTest` (e.g.,\n    `app/src/test/java/com/example/data/repository/*RepositoryRuleTest.kt` or\n    `*RuleTest.kt`) extending `FirestoreEmulatorTestBase` (see\n    [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)).\n    Exercising the actual Kotlin repository methods directly against the\n    emulator guarantees collection path alignment and prevents schema\n    serialization bugs (such as empty string vs. null sentinel mismatches).\n4.  **Execute Local Tests (Two-Tier Sequential Testing)**: The Auth (port 9099)\n    and Firestore (port 8085) emulators are **NOT running** when the container\n    starts; tests run before starting them fail with `TypeError: fetch failed`.\n    Start them once, detached, then keep them warm. Never use `firebase\n    emulators:exec` in tight loops (it re-spins the emulator JVM on every run).\n    Run Gradle with a warm daemon rather than `--no-daemon` to reduce\n    incremental test latency from 25 seconds to 1\342\200\2233 seconds. Filter execution\n    with `--tests \'*Rule*\'` to skip unrelated Roborazzi Compose screenshot\n    tests.\n\n    -   **Tier 0: Start Emulators Once (Detached) \342\200\224 RUN BEFORE ANY TEST**: After\n        writing `firebase.json` (item 5) and `firestore.rules`, run the\n        idempotent detached start command from\n        [references/firestore-rules-testing.md \302\247 Start the Emulators Once](references/firestore-rules-testing.md#start-the-emulators-once-warm-detached)\n        from the workspace root and confirm it prints `Emulators ready`. Do not\n        restart between test runs; rules edits hot-reload.\n\n    -   **Tier 1: JavaScript Rules Validation (`firestore.test.js`) \342\200\224 RUN\n        FIRST**: Validate `firestore.rules` syntax, unauthenticated access\n        rejection, and cross-user isolation in ~1 second with zero Gradle build\n        overhead:\n\n        ```bash\n        FIRESTORE_EMULATOR_HOST=\"127.0.0.1:8085\" node --test firestore.test.js\n        ```\n\n    -   **Tier 2: Kotlin Repository Tests (`*RuleTest.kt`) \342\200\224 RUN AFTER RULES\n        VALIDATE**: Once security rules validate in JavaScript, execute\n        Android-native Robolectric repository tests to verify Kotlin data\n        models, Flow emissions, and repository methods against the emulator:\n\n        ```bash\n        gradle :app:testDebugUnitTest --tests \'*Rule*\'\n        ```\n5.  **Emulator Multi-Database Rule Binding (`firebase.json`)**: The Firestore\n    emulator binds `firestore.rules` on startup only to databases declared in\n    `firebase.json`. When `app/src/main/res/values/firebase_applet_config.xml`\n    is present, `firebase.json` must use the array form targeting the named\n    database ID (`firestore_database_id`) \342\200\224 see\n    [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)\n    for the exact file contents.\n\n    If the named database is omitted from `firebase.json`, the Firestore\n    emulator creates it implicitly with open rules, bypassing security rule\n    evaluations during local tests. (Note: For live backend deployment,\n    `deploy_firebase` automatically resolves the named database directly from\n    `firebase_applet_config.xml`.)\n6.  **Deploy Verified Rules (MANDATORY)**: Always invoke the `deploy_firebase`\n    tool to push the verified rules to the backend for every app that uses\n    Firestore. If invoking `deploy_firebase` fails due to an unprovisioned\n    sandbox, authorization error, or missing project credentials, log the\n    constraint and **stop immediately without retrying in a loop**.\n\n### Step 2: Google Sign-In with Jetpack Credential Manager\n\n-   **Dependencies**: Require all 4 Auth dependencies in `app/build.gradle.kts`\n    (`firebase.auth`, `androidx.credentials`,\n    `androidx.credentials.play.services`, `googleid`).\n-   **Interactive Sign-In (`onGoogleSignInClicked`)**: MUST use\n    `GetSignInWithGoogleOption.Builder(serverClientId = clientId).build()`.\n-   **Silent Auto Sign-In (`attemptAutoSignIn`)**: Use\n    `GetGoogleIdOption.Builder().setFilterByAuthorizedAccounts(true).setServerClientId(clientId).setAutoSelectEnabled(true).build()`\n    exclusively for background or startup credential checks.\n-   **Mandatory Interactive Fallback**: Always provide a visible \"Sign in with\n    Google\" button (or fallback auth card) using `GetSignInWithGoogleOption`.\n    When background or silent auto-sign-in fails (e.g. `NoCredentialException`\n    on clean devices or emulators), the user can still sign in interactively.\n    Never omit the interactive fallback.\n-   **One Option Per Request & Cancellation Safety**: Build each\n    `GetCredentialRequest` with exactly one credential option \342\200\224\n    `GetSignInWithGoogleOption` for interactive sign-in, and `GetGoogleIdOption`\n    in a separate request for silent auto sign-in. Supplying one option per\n    request keeps the Credential Manager bottom sheet responsive on Android 16\n    (API 36) emulators and devices, where a single request carrying both options\n    leaves the sheet unresponsive. Catch `GetCredentialCancellationException`\n    separately from other exceptions: log a diagnostic warning (`Log.w(\"Auth\",\n    \"Google Sign-In cancelled or dismissed: ${e.message}\", e)`) to retain\n    observability for credential or configuration mismatches (such as missing\n    SHA-1 fingerprints or invalid Web Client IDs), and reset transient UI state\n-   **Web Client ID Resolution**: `R.string.default_web_client_id` is\n    automatically generated by the Google Services Gradle plugin from\n    `google-services.json` at build time. Retrieve it via\n    `context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)` (do not manually define\n    it in `strings.xml` or use reflection via `getIdentifier`).\n-   **Local & Emulator Sign-In Testing Bridge**: `CredentialManager` requires\n    Google Play services dialogs, which are absent in headless unit/Robolectric\n    test environments (throwing `NoCredentialException`). To test Firestore\n    rules and repository data layers locally without altering production\n    `CredentialManager` code, authenticate tests against the local Auth emulator\n    (port 9099) via `auth.signInWithEmailAndPassword(...)` or\n    `auth.createUserWithEmailAndPassword(...)` (see\n    [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)).\n\nInclude a visible \"Sign in with Google\" button and implement both interactive\nand silent `CredentialManager` flows in your application:\n\n```kotlin\n// Note: `R` resolves from your application\'s own package. If this code lives in\n// a subpackage, import it explicitly: import <your.application.package>.R\n\n// 1. Silent Auto-Sign-In on App Startup (Call in onCreate/onStart or initial Composable launch)\nfun attemptAutoSignIn(\n  context: Context,\n  credentialManager: CredentialManager,\n  onAuthSuccess: () -> Unit,\n  onUnauthenticated: () -> Unit,\n  scope: CoroutineScope\n) {\n  if (Firebase.auth.currentUser != null) {\n    onAuthSuccess()\n    return\n  }\n  val clientId = try {\n    context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)\n  } catch (e: Exception) {\n    onUnauthenticated()\n    return\n  }\n\n  val googleIdOption = GetGoogleIdOption.Builder()\n    .setFilterByAuthorizedAccounts(true)\n    .setServerClientId(clientId)\n    .setAutoSelectEnabled(true)\n    .build()\n\n  val request = GetCredentialRequest.Builder().addCredentialOption(googleIdOption).build()\n\n  scope.launch {\n    try {\n      val result = credentialManager.getCredential(context, request)\n      val credential = result.credential\n      if (credential is CustomCredential && credential.type == TYPE_GOOGLE_ID_TOKEN_CREDENTIAL) {\n        val googleIdToken = GoogleIdTokenCredential.createFrom(credential.data).idToken\n        val authCredential = GoogleAuthProvider.getCredential(googleIdToken, null)\n        Firebase.auth.signInWithCredential(authCredential).await()\n        onAuthSuccess()\n      } else {\n        onUnauthenticated()\n      }\n    } catch (e: Exception) {\n      onUnauthenticated()\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// 2. Interactive Sign-In Button Component\n@Composable\nfun GoogleSignInButton(\n  onAuthSuccess: () -> Unit,\n  onAuthError: (String) -> Unit\n) {\n  val context = LocalContext.current\n  val coroutineScope = rememberCoroutineScope()\n  val credentialManager = CredentialManager.create(context)\n  var isLoading by remember { mutableStateOf(false) }\n\n  Button(\n    onClick = {\n      isLoading = true\n      onGoogleSignInClicked(\n        context,\n        credentialManager,\n        onAuthSuccess = {\n          isLoading = false\n          onAuthSuccess()\n        },\n        onAuthError = { errorMsg ->\n          isLoading = false\n          onAuthError(errorMsg)\n        },\n        coroutineScope,\n        onAuthCancelled = { isLoading = false }\n      )\n    },\n    enabled = !isLoading\n  ) {\n    if (isLoading) {\n      CircularProgressIndicator(\n        modifier = Modifier.size(24.dp),\n        color = MaterialTheme.colorScheme.onPrimary,\n        strokeWidth = 2.dp\n      )\n    } else {\n      Text(\"Sign in with Google\")\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfun onGoogleSignInClicked(\n  context: Context,\n  credentialManager: CredentialManager,\n  onAuthSuccess: () -> Unit,\n  onAuthError: (String) -> Unit,\n  scope: CoroutineScope,\n  onAuthCancelled: () -> Unit = {}\n) {\n  val clientId = try {\n    context.getString(R.string.default_web_client_id)\n  } catch (e: Exception) {\n    onAuthError(\"Google Sign-In configuration missing: default_web_client_id not found\")\n    return\n  }\n\n  val signInOption = GetSignInWithGoogleOption.Builder(serverClientId = clientId).build()\n  val request = GetCredentialRequest.Builder().addCredentialOption(signInOption).build()\n\n  scope.launch {\n    try {\n      val result = credentialManager.getCredential(context as Activity, request)\n      val credential = result.credential\n      if (credential is CustomCredential && credential.type == TYPE_GOOGLE_ID_TOKEN_CREDENTIAL) {\n        val googleIdToken = GoogleIdTokenCredential.createFrom(credential.data).idToken\n        val authCredential = GoogleAuthProvider.getCredential(googleIdToken, null)\n        Firebase.auth.signInWithCredential(authCredential).await()\n        onAuthSuccess()\n      } else {\n        onAuthError(\"Unexpected credential type\")\n      }\n    } catch (e: GetCredentialCancellationException) {\n      Log.w(\"Auth\", \"Google Sign-In flow cancelled or dismissed: ${e.message}\", e)\n      onAuthCancelled()\n    } catch (e: Exception) {\n      Log.e(\"Auth\", \"Google Sign-In failed\", e)\n      onAuthError(e.localizedMessage ?: \"Sign in failed\")\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. Sign-Out Flow (Must sign out of Firebase AND clear Credential Manager state)\nfun signOut(\n  context: Context,\n  credentialManager: CredentialManager,\n  onSignOutComplete: () -> Unit,\n  scope: CoroutineScope\n) {\n  Firebase.auth.signOut()\n  scope.launch {\n    try {\n      credentialManager.clearCredentialState(ClearCredentialStateRequest())\n    } catch (e: Exception) {\n      Log.e(\"Auth\", \"Failed to clear credential state\", e)\n    } finally {\n      onSignOutComplete()\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// 4. UI Layer Auth Gating in MainActivity.kt / Root Composable\n// NOTE: If AI Logic is also requested, invoke `view_file` on\n// `../firebase-ai-logic-android/references/app-check.md`. That file is the\n// authoritative App Check contract: it carries the `configureAppCheck(context,\n// intent)` helper implementation plus the call-site ordering required here.\n// Call the helper at the beginning of `MainActivity.onCreate()` (before\n// `setContent` or initializing ViewModels). `configureAppCheck` is a project\n// helper function, not an SDK built-in, so the agent will not have its\n// implementation without reading that reference.\nclass MainActivity : ComponentActivity() {\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\n\n        // CO-PRESENCE WITH AI LOGIC: If AI Logic is also requested, invoke\n        // view_file on ../firebase-ai-logic-android/references/app-check.md to\n        // implement and call configureAppCheck(this, intent) first.\n        // configureAppCheck(this, intent)\n\n        setContent {\n            MaterialTheme {\n                AppContent()\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n@Composable\nfun AppContent() {\n  var currentUser by remember { mutableStateOf(Firebase.auth.currentUser) }\n\n  DisposableEffect(Unit) {\n    val listener = FirebaseAuth.AuthStateListener { auth ->\n      currentUser = auth.currentUser\n    }\n    Firebase.auth.addAuthStateListener(listener)\n    onDispose {\n      Firebase.auth.removeAuthStateListener(listener)\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (currentUser == null) {\n    // Render login UI with Google Sign-In button\n    AuthScreen(\n      onAuthSuccess = { currentUser = Firebase.auth.currentUser }\n    )\n  } else {\n    // Authenticated user session; render main feature screens & repositories\n    MainFeatureScreen(\n      currentUserId = currentUser!!.uid\n    )\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Step 3: Custom Database Instance (`R.string.firestore_database_id`) & Authenticated Data Operations\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] **REPOSITORY AUTHENTICATION DELEGATION**\n>\n> Manage user authentication exclusively within the UI layer (`MainActivity` /\n> `AuthScreen`). Repositories must never self-authenticate or attempt to call\n> `signInAnonymously()`. In the repository layer, enforce session state using\n> fail-fast assertions such as `requireUserId()` for write payload construction\n> or accept `userId: String` passed down from the ViewModel.\n\nResolve `context.getString(R.string.firestore_database_id)` at the\ndependency-injection boundary and pass the configured `FirebaseFirestore` into\nrepositories (see\n[references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md#android-lifecycle--state-integration)).\nExplicitly scope operations to the authenticated user\'s ID:\n\n```kotlin\n@IgnoreExtraProperties\ndata class Item(\n    @DocumentId val id: String = \"\",\n    val name: String = \"\",\n    val quantity: Long = 0L,\n    @ServerTimestamp val createdAt: Timestamp? = null,\n    @ServerTimestamp val updatedAt: Timestamp? = null\n)\n\nclass AppRepository(private val db: FirebaseFirestore) {\n    // CRITICAL: Always initialize with R.string.firestore_database_id\n    constructor(context: Context) : this(\n        FirebaseFirestore.getInstance(\n            context.applicationContext.getString(R.string.firestore_database_id)\n        )\n    )\n\n    private val auth = Firebase.auth\n\n    // Direct fail-fast accessor for authenticated Google user\'s UID\n    private fun requireUserId(): String {\n        return auth.currentUser?.uid\n            ?: throw IllegalStateException(\"User must be signed in with Google before accessing Firestore.\")\n    }\n\n    // Real-time observation using first-party snapshots() Flow\n    fun observeItems(): Flow<List<Item>> = flow {\n        val uid = requireUserId()\n        val path = \"users/$uid/items\"\n        emitAll(\n            db.collection(\"users\").document(uid).collection(\"items\")\n                .snapshots()\n                .map { snapshot -> snapshot.toObjects(Item::class.java) }\n                .catch { error ->\n                    if (error is Exception) handleFirestoreError(error, OperationType.LIST, path)\n                    throw error\n                }\n        )\n    }\n\n    // Update existing document using its populated document ID\n    suspend fun updateItem(item: Item) {\n        val uid = requireUserId()\n        require(item.id.isNotBlank()) { \"Item ID must not be blank for update\" }\n        requireNotNull(item.createdAt) { \"Item createdAt must not be null for update\" }\n        val docRef = db.collection(\"users\").document(uid).collection(\"items\").document(item.id)\n        try {\n            // Setting updatedAt = null triggers @ServerTimestamp to generate a new server timestamp,\n            // while preserving existing non-null createdAt so security rules do not reject the update.\n            docRef.set(item.copy(updatedAt = null)).await()\n        } catch (e: CancellationException) {\n            throw e\n        } catch (e: Exception) {\n            handleFirestoreError(e, OperationType.UPDATE, docRef.path)\n            throw e\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Use FieldValue.serverTimestamp() for write timestamps and null-safe Timestamp? for models\n    // Note: handleFirestoreError and OperationType are defined in references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md\n    fun addItem(itemData: Map<String, Any>, onError: (String) -> Unit = {}) {\n        val uid = requireUserId()\n        val itemsRef = db.collection(\"users\").document(uid).collection(\"items\")\n        val payload = itemData + mapOf(\n            \"userId\" to uid,\n            \"createdAt\" to FieldValue.serverTimestamp(),\n            \"updatedAt\" to FieldValue.serverTimestamp()\n        )\n        itemsRef.add(payload)\n            // MANDATORY: never drop a Firestore failure. handleFirestoreError logs the\n            // structured JSON diagnostic; pass a user-friendly message to UI state.\n            .addOnFailureListener { exception ->\n                handleFirestoreError(exception, OperationType.CREATE, itemsRef.path)\n                onError(exception.localizedMessage ?: \"Failed to add item\")\n            }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 6. Core Operational Invariants\n\nTo prevent system failures and security breaches, you MUST strictly obey these\nuniversal rules:\n\n1.  **Verified Rule Deployment (Never Deploy Untested Rules)**: Whenever\n    `firestore.rules` is created or modified, **both test tiers MUST execute and\n    pass** before calling `deploy_firebase` \342\200\224 rapid JavaScript rules tests\n    first, then Android-native Robolectric repository tests. Deploying\n    unverified rules is strictly forbidden. Refer to\n    [references/firestore-rules-testing.md](references/firestore-rules-testing.md)\n    for the exact commands, `FirestoreEmulatorTestBase`, looper configuration,\n    and bottleneck mitigations (warm daemons, dynamic document IDs /\n    `clearFirestore()`, `failFast = true`).\n2.  **Mandatory Structured Error Handling**: All Firestore operations (`get()`,\n    `set()`, `add()`, `update()`, `delete()`, listeners, queries) **MUST** catch\n    errors using `handleFirestoreError(exception, operationType, path)` from\n    [references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md](references/error-handling-and-pipelines.md).\n3.  **Scoped Standard Queries**: Always scope queries to the authenticated\n    user\'s ID (`whereEqualTo(\"userId\", uid)` or document subcollections under\n    `users/{uid}`) and attach realtime listeners inside Coroutine flows.\n4.  **Mandatory Server Timestamps & Null-Safety**: Write\n    `FieldValue.serverTimestamp()`, never `Timestamp.now()`; declare timestamp\n    fields as nullable `Timestamp?`. Refer to\n    [references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md#mandatory-server-timestamps--null-safety)\n    for the full null-safety rules, including\n    `ServerTimestampBehavior.ESTIMATE`.\n5.  **Mandatory Type-Safe Deserialization & Number Coercion**:\n    *   All Kotlin `data class` models used with Firestore **MUST provide\n        default values for every property** to ensure the synthetic no-arg\n        constructor required by `.toObject()` is generated.\n    *   Annotate the document identifier property with `@DocumentId val id:\n        String = \"\"` to automatically map the document ID on read and exclude it\n        from written document fields.\n    *   Use `@ServerTimestamp val createdAt: Timestamp? = null` for automatic\n        server-side timestamps on typed models.\n    *   When reading raw maps (`Map<String, Any?>`) or `doc.get()`, convert\n        numeric values safely via `(data[\"field\"] as? Number)?.toInt() ?: 0` or\n        SDK typed getters (`doc.getLong()`, `doc.getDouble()`) to accommodate\n        Firestore\'s `java.lang.Long` representation. Refer to\n        [references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md#type-safe-kotlin-data-models--document-id-mapping)\n        for complete code templates.\n6.  **Mandatory Client Query Alignment (rules are not filters)**: If a rule\n    restricts list access based on document fields, the client query MUST\n    include the corresponding filters (e.g., `.whereEqualTo(\"field\", value)`).\n    Resolve `PERMISSION_DENIED` by aligning the query or denormalizing the\n    required authorization field \342\200\224 **never** by weakening the rule, and never at\n    the cost of user isolation or authentication checks.\n7.  **Denormalize Auth Metadata for Subcollections**: For high-frequency\n    subcollections (e.g., list items), prefer denormalizing authorization\n    metadata (e.g., `memberUids`) directly onto subcollection documents to avoid\n    transitive `get()` lookups to parent documents and associated latency.\n8.  **Partial Updates & Post-Write State**: In Firestore security rules,\n    `request.resource.data` represents the projected post-write document state\n    after applying changes. Required fields remain present during partial\n    updates and `SetOptions.merge()`, so they must be directly asserted in\n    `isValid[Entity]()`, while optional fields permit absence (`!(\'field\' in\n    data) || ...`). Server transforms (`FieldValue.increment()`) resolve before\n    rule evaluation, enabling standard type checks.\n9.  **Mandatory Auth Gating for Collection Queries & Realtime Listeners**: Never\n    execute Firestore queries or attach snapshot listeners when unauthenticated.\n    Root navigation MUST gate feature screens behind authentication\n    (`AppNavigation`), ensuring ViewModels and queries are not instantiated on\n    cold start before user login. ViewModels must log stream errors rather than\n    failing silently. Refer to\n    [references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md](references/sdk-and-lifecycle.md).\n10. **Two-Tier Kotlin Flow Error Handling Architecture**: Prefer first-party\n    `Query.snapshots()` and `DocumentReference.snapshots()` as the primary\n    standard for real-time Firestore observation. `snapshots()` registers the\n    snapshot listener on collection, removes it when collection stops, and\n    terminates the flow with the underlying exception on error. When bridging a\n    callback API manually with `callbackFlow`, call `close(error)` on failure so\n    `awaitClose { registration.remove() }` runs and releases the listener\n    registration. Because stream errors propagate downstream as exceptions,\n    ViewModels MUST map upstream emissions to the UI state hierarchy before\n    catching errors: chain `.map<List<Item>, UiState<List<Item>>> {\n    UiState.Success(it) }` before `.catch { emit(UiState.Error(it.message ?:\n    \"Failed to observe items\")) }` prior to `.stateIn(...)` to protect Jetpack\n    Compose collectors (`collectAsStateWithLifecycle()`) from fatal stream\n    crashes.\n\n    ```kotlin\n    val itemsState: StateFlow<UiState<List<Item>>> = repository.observeItems()\n        .map<List<Item>, UiState<List<Item>>> { UiState.Success(it) }\n        .catch { emit(UiState.Error(it.message ?: \"Failed to observe items\")) }\n        .stateIn(\n            scope = viewModelScope,\n            started = SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5000L),\n            initialValue = UiState.Loading\n        )\n    ```\n\n    Alternatively, repositories can expose `Flow<Result<T>>` emitting\n    `Result.failure(error)` before completing.\n11. **Credential Manager Request Construction**: Build each\n    `GetCredentialRequest` with exactly one credential option \342\200\224 use\n    `GetSignInWithGoogleOption` for interactive sign-in and `GetGoogleIdOption`\n    in a separate request for silent auto sign-in \342\200\224 which keeps the bottom sheet\n    responsive on Android API 36. Catch `GetCredentialCancellationException`\n    separately from other exceptions: log a diagnostic warning (`Log.w(\"Auth\",\n    \"Google Sign-In cancelled or dismissed: ${e.message}\", e)`) to retain\n    observability for credential or configuration mismatches (such as missing\n    SHA-1 fingerprints or invalid Web Client IDs), and reset transient UI state\n    (`isLoading = false`) so the user can retry.\n"
    num_lines: 775
    num_bytes: 38363
  }
}
